d3d11: SV_PrimitiveID written by a geometry shader

Applies on top of 0012.

A geometry shader that writes SV_PrimitiveID (dcl_output_sgv) hit an
assert, so a release build dropped the output, and the pixel shader's
SV_PrimitiveID stayed Metal's [[primitive_id]]: behind a mesh function
that is the primitive's index inside its threadgroup, not the value the
geometry shader wrote (d3d11-tess-probe gs-primitive-id-out 66.72 % off
WARP).  The same fix as SPIRV-Cross 0130 / MoltenVK 0131 for Vulkan:

- The geometry shader stores the value as a flat per-vertex mesh
  attribute, "anyos_primitive_id" (uint).  A per-primitive user attribute
  would be the natural carrier, but it crashes Apple's MTLCompilerService
  on M1 (found with 0130).  Such a shader is never treated as a
  pass-through geometry shader, and its reflection says
  WritesPrimitiveID.
- The pixel shader gets a variant (SM50_SHADER_PSO_PIXEL_SHADER_DATA::
  gs_primitive_id, ShaderVariantPixel::gs_primitive_id) that the geometry
  pipeline selects when its geometry shader writes SV_PrimitiveID; it reads
  the attribute instead of [[primitive_id]].  The [[primitive_id]]
  argument stays declared, so the argument order of every other pixel
  shader is unchanged.
- Cache identity: the new pixel-shader field is hashed only when set, so
  every other pixel shader keeps its function name and cache entry; the
  geometry-shader variant digest takes a constant so conversions cached
  by an earlier build are not replayed.

Without a geometry shader, and for geometry shaders that do not write
SV_PrimitiveID, the generated code is unchanged.

